{"version": "6.9", "hitCount": 6, "nextCursorMark": "AoIIPShbOSg0MTU4MjYyMQ==", "nextPageUrl": "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=%22SOX9%22+AND+lacrimal+AND+%22Homo+sapiens%22%5BOrganism%5D&cursorMark=AoIIPShbOSg0MTU4MjYyMQ%3D%3D&resultType=core&pageSize=5&format=json", "request": {"queryString": "\"SOX9\" AND lacrimal AND \"Homo sapiens\"[Organism]", "resultType": "core", "cursorMark": "*", "pageSize": 5, "sort": "", "synonym": false}, "resultList": {"result": [{"id": "33329706", "source": "MED", "pmid": "33329706", "pmcid": "PMC7719703", "fullTextIdList": {"fullTextId": ["PMC7719703"]}, "doi": "10.3389/fgene.2020.569314", "title": "Transcription Factors of the Alx Family: Evolutionarily Conserved Regulators of Deuterostome Skeletogenesis.", "authorString": "Khor JM, Ettensohn CA.", "authorList": {"author": [{"fullName": "Khor JM", "lastName": "Khor", "firstName": "Jian Ming", "initials": "JM", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-1428-6770"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Biological Sciences, Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, PA, United States."}]}}, {"fullName": "Ettensohn CA", "lastName": "Ettensohn", "firstName": "Charles A", "initials": "CA", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Biological Sciences, Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, PA, United States."}]}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-1428-6770"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"volume": "11", "journalIssueId": 2914987, "dateOfPublication": "2020 ", "monthOfPublication": 0, "yearOfPublication": 2020, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Front Genet", "title": "Frontiers in genetics", "essn": "1664-8021", "issn": "1664-8021", "isoabbreviation": "Front Genet", "nlmid": "101560621"}, "printPublicationDate": "2020-01-01"}, "pubYear": "2020", "pageInfo": "569314", "abstractText": "Members of the <i>alx</i> gene family encode transcription factors that contain a highly conserved Paired-class, DNA-binding homeodomain, and a C-terminal OAR/Aristaless domain. Phylogenetic and comparative genomic studies have revealed complex patterns of <i>alx</i> gene duplications during deuterostome evolution. Remarkably, <i>alx</i> genes have been implicated in skeletogenesis in both echinoderms and vertebrates. In this review, we provide an overview of current knowledge concerning <i>alx</i> genes in deuterostomes. We highlight their evolutionarily conserved role in skeletogenesis and draw parallels and distinctions between the skeletogenic gene regulatory circuitries of diverse groups within the superphylum.", "affiliation": "Department of Biological Sciences, Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, PA, United States.", "publicationStatus": "epublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Electronic-eCollection", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["review-article", "Review", "Journal Article"]}, "keywordList": {"keyword": ["Biomineralization", "Osteogenesis", "Chondrogenesis", "calcification", "Skeletogenesis", "Neural Crest Cell", "Deuterostome Evolution", "Alx Transcription Factors"]}, "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Subscription required", "availabilityCode": "S", "documentStyle": "doi", "site": "DOI", "url": "https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2020.569314"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "html", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC7719703"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "pdf", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC7719703?pdf=render"}]}, "isOpenAccess": "Y", "inEPMC": "Y", "inPMC": "Y", "citedByCount": 18, "hasReferences": "Y", "hasTextMinedTerms": "Y", "hasDbCrossReferences": "N", "hasPDF": "Y", "hasBook": "N", "hasSuppl": "N", "hasLabsLinks": "N", "hasData": "Y", "license": "cc by", "authMan": "N", "epmcAuthMan": "N", "nihAuthMan": "N", "hasTMAccessionNumbers": "Y", "tmAccessionTypeList": {"accessionType": ["doi"]}, "dateOfCreation": "2020-12-17", "dateOfRevision": "2020-12-18", "electronicPublicationDate": "2020-11-23", "firstPublicationDate": "2020-11-23", "firstIndexDate": "2020-12-24", "fullTextReceivedDate": "2025-02-03", "hasEvaluations": "N"}, {"id": "33375093", "source": "MED", "pmid": "33375093", "pmcid": "PMC7822183", "fullTextIdList": {"fullTextId": ["PMC7822183"]}, "doi": "10.3390/biom11010002", "title": "Molecules, Mechanisms, and Disorders of Self-Domestication: Keys for Understanding Emotional and Social Communication from an Evolutionary Perspective.", "authorString": "\u0160imi\u0107 G, Vuki\u0107 V, Kopi\u0107 J, Krsnik \u017d, Hof PR.", "authorList": {"author": [{"fullName": "\u0160imi\u0107 G", "lastName": "\u0160imi\u0107", "firstName": "Goran", "initials": "G", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-6339-9261"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Neuroscience, Croatian Institute for Brain Research, University of Zagreb Medical School, 10000 Zagreb, Croatia."}]}}, {"fullName": "Vuki\u0107 V", "lastName": "Vuki\u0107", "firstName": "Vana", "initials": "V", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Neuroscience, Croatian Institute for Brain Research, University of Zagreb Medical School, 10000 Zagreb, Croatia."}]}}, {"fullName": "Kopi\u0107 J", "lastName": "Kopi\u0107", "firstName": "Janja", "initials": "J", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Neuroscience, Croatian Institute for Brain Research, University of Zagreb Medical School, 10000 Zagreb, Croatia."}]}}, {"fullName": "Krsnik \u017d", "lastName": "Krsnik", "firstName": "\u017deljka", "initials": "\u017d", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-5238-6820"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Neuroscience, Croatian Institute for Brain Research, University of Zagreb Medical School, 10000 Zagreb, Croatia."}]}}, {"fullName": "Hof PR", "lastName": "Hof", "firstName": "Patrick R", "initials": "PR", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-3208-1154"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Nash Family Department of Neuroscience, Friedman Brain Institute, and Ronald M. Loeb Center for Alzheimer's disease, Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai, New York, NY 10029, USA."}]}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-5238-6820"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-3208-1154"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-6339-9261"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "1", "volume": "11", "journalIssueId": 3080756, "dateOfPublication": "2020 Dec", "monthOfPublication": 12, "yearOfPublication": 2020, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Biomolecules", "title": "Biomolecules", "essn": "2218-273X", "issn": "2218-273X", "isoabbreviation": "Biomolecules", "nlmid": "101596414"}, "printPublicationDate": "2020-12-01"}, "pubYear": "2020", "pageInfo": "E2", "abstractText": "The neural crest hypothesis states that the phenotypic features of the domestication syndrome are due to a reduced number or disruption of neural crest cells (NCCs) migration, as these cells differentiate at their final destinations and proliferate into different tissues whose activity is reduced by domestication. Comparing the phenotypic characteristics of modern and prehistoric man, it is clear that during their recent evolutionary past, humans also went through a process of self-domestication with a simultaneous prolongation of the period of socialization. This has led to the development of social abilities and skills, especially language, as well as neoteny. Disorders of neural crest cell development and migration lead to many different conditions such as Waardenburg syndrome, Hirschsprung disease, fetal alcohol syndrome, DiGeorge and Treacher-Collins syndrome, for which the mechanisms are already relatively well-known. However, for others, such as Williams-Beuren syndrome and schizophrenia that have the characteristics of hyperdomestication, and autism spectrum disorders, and 7dupASD syndrome that have the characteristics of hypodomestication, much less is known. Thus, deciphering the biological determinants of disordered self-domestication has great potential for elucidating the normal and disturbed ontogenesis of humans, as well as for the understanding of evolution of mammals in general.", "affiliation": "Department of Neuroscience, Croatian Institute for Brain Research, University of Zagreb Medical School, 10000 Zagreb, Croatia.", "publicationStatus": "epublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "review-article", "Review", "Journal Article"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "Hrvatska Zaklada za Znanost", "grantId": "2014-09-9730", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Hrvatska Zaklada za Znanost", "grantId": "2019-04-3584", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "European Regional Development Fund", "grantId": "KK01.1.1.01.0007", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Neural Crest", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "GD", "qualifierName": "growth & development"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "ME", "qualifierName": "metabolism"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "PP", "qualifierName": "physiopathology"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Mandibulofacial Dysostosis", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "GE", "qualifierName": "genetics"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Hirschsprung Disease", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"maj